    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d51">

                <pb facs="0128" n="40" sameAs="frus:frus1933v04/pg40"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.G Personnel/322: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chairman of the
                        American Delegation to the Seventh International Conference of American
                        States</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hull</hi>)<anchor n="36"
                        xml:id="d51-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-11-24T20:00:00-05:00">November 24, 1933—8
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>15. The Spanish Chargé d’Affaires, under instructions, inquired yesterday whether
                    there would be any objection on our part to the presence of a Spanish observer
                    at the Montevideo Conference.</p>
                <p>In reply I referred him to the regulations limiting Pan American Conferences to
                    representatives of Latin American States, etc., and said that, personally in the
                    circumstances, I did not feel that it was proper for the United States to take
                    any individual position in the matter. I added that, inasmuch as you are about
                    to arrive at Montevideo, his Government would naturally desire to approach you
                    direct, should they care to pursue the inquiry any further.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d51-f1" n="36" type="source" xml:id="d51-f1-content">
                <p>Mr. Hull was aboard the S. S. <hi rendition="#italic">American Legion</hi>, at
                    sea.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
